mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Habit Streak Tracker API\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis backend stores a private, per-user clean-streak record for a habit-tracking app. It tracks the current running streak, a history of completed streaks with trigger tags, milestone thresholds, and a daily check-in flag. All data is scoped to the calling principal; no user can read or write another user's data.\n\n## Authentication and Authorization\n\nEvery public method is a `shared` update function that reads the caller's principal from `{ caller }`. There is no anonymous access path: a call made by the anonymous principal is treated as its own distinct user and receives its own empty state, so it can never see a signed-in user's data.\n\nData is keyed by the caller principal in a `Map<Principal, StreakState>`. Each caller only ever reads and mutates the entry for their own principal. There is no admin or cross-user read endpoint.\n\nA caller's principal is derived by the app's frontend against its pinned Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin`. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires. A principal derived against a different origin is a different user and sees a different (empty) state.\n\n## Methods\n\n- `getStreakSummary() : async StreakSummary` — returns the caller's summary. Creates an empty state on first call. Read-only in effect but declared as an update because it may create the state entry.\n- `startStreak() : async StreakSummary` — starts a fresh clean streak for the caller if none is running. If a streak is already running, it is left unchanged. Returns the updated summary.\n- `checkInToday() : async StreakSummary` — marks the caller's running streak as checked in at the current time. If no streak is running, one is started at the current time and marked checked in. Returns the updated summary.\n- `relapse(triggers : [Text]) : async StreakSummary` — saves the caller's running streak to history with the given trigger tags, then immediately starts a fresh streak. Returns the updated summary.\n\n## Types and Units\n\n- `Timestamp` is `Int` nanoseconds since the Unix epoch (`Time.now()`).\n- `Streak.startedAt` is the streak start in nanoseconds; `lastCheckInAt` is the most recent check-in in nanoseconds, or `null` if never checked in.\n- `StreakLog.durationSeconds` is a whole number of seconds (nanoseconds divided by 1,000,000,000).\n- `StreakSummary.totalCleanSeconds` and `longestStreakSeconds` are whole seconds.\n- `StreakSummary.milestones` is a list of `{ days : Nat; earned : Bool }` for the fixed thresholds `[1, 3, 7, 14, 30, 90]` days. A milestone is earned when the longest streak reaches its day count.\n- `StreakSummary.checkedInToday` is `true` when the last check-in falls on the same whole-day bucket as the current time.\n- `StreakSummary.logs` is the full history array, newest last.\n- `relapse` trigger tags are free-form `Text` values supplied by the caller.\n\n## Lifecycle and Polling\n\nA user's state begins empty. `getStreakSummary` or `startStreak` creates it. A running streak has a `startedAt` and an optional `lastCheckInAt`. `checkInToday` updates the check-in time. `relapse` appends a completed log and resets the current streak to start now.\n\nThere is no server-side timer or background job. The live streak duration is computed from `startedAt` at call time, so the frontend should poll `getStreakSummary` on its own interval to refresh the displayed duration. `checkedInToday` is computed from the current time, so it can change across a day boundary without any write.\n\n## Retry Safety\n\n- `getStreakSummary` is idempotent apart from creating an empty state on first call.\n- `startStreak` is idempotent while a streak is running: repeated calls do not reset `startedAt`.\n- `checkInToday` is idempotent in effect: repeated calls within the same day update the check-in timestamp but do not change the day bucket.\n- `relapse` is NOT idempotent. Each call appends a new history log and starts a new streak. A retried `relapse` creates a duplicate log entry. The frontend must guard against duplicate submissions.\n\n## Errors and Limits\n\nThere are no caller-visible error variants; the methods always return a summary. The only failure mode is a trap from an internal invariant. History grows without bound in this version, so very long-lived accounts accumulate logs.";
  };
};
